"""Flashcard format (hint + the math + airplane translation + breakdown): CMO topics 09-13."""
B = {
901: (r"Which walls is the plane actually touching, and which can it ignore?",
  [r"<strong>$h_i(x)=0$ and $g_j(x)\le0$</strong> define the legal airspace $\Omega$: equality walls you must stay on, inequality walls you must stay behind.",
   r"<strong>$\mathcal A(x)=\{j:g_j(x)=0\}$ = the walls you are pressed against right now.</strong>",
   r"<strong>Walls with $g_j\lt0$ are far away:</strong> in a small ball around $x$ they never matter."]),
902: (r"What is the plane's velocity at the instant of touchdown?",
  [r"<strong>$\dot x(t^*)$ is the limit of displacement over time.</strong> Dividing $x(t)-x^*$ by $t-t^*$ turns a small step into a velocity.",
   r"<strong>$M=\{y:\nabla h(x^*)^\top y=0\}$ = the tangent plane:</strong> every velocity perpendicular to the wall normal, i.e. along the wall.",
   r"<strong>Equality constraints allow only these sliding directions.</strong>"]),
903: (r"Which headings can the plane really take off along from its parked position?",
  [r"<strong>$x_k\in\Omega$, $t_k\downarrow0$:</strong> a sequence of legal points closing in on $x$ from inside the airspace.",
   r"<strong>$\frac{x_k-x}{t_k}\to d$:</strong> dividing by the shrinking step $t_k$ turns the tiny displacement into a heading $d$.",
   r"<strong>The set of all such $d$ is a cone,</strong> not a subspace: you can scale a safe heading but not reverse it."]),
905: (r"Why must every legal heading go flat or uphill at the lowest parking spot?",
  [r"<strong>$\nabla f(x^*)^\top d$ = the altitude change per unit leg along $d$.</strong>",
   r"<strong>It must be $\ge0$ for every legal $d\in T_\Omega(x^*)$:</strong> if some legal heading went downhill, releasing the brakes would lower the altitude.",
   r"<strong>$\nabla f(x^*)$ is usually not zero here:</strong> the wall is what stops the plane, not flat ground."]),
906: (r"When can the plane trust the straight-line map of the walls?",
  [r"<strong>$L_\Omega$ replaces each curved wall by its tangent line at $x^*$:</strong> the Nav Computer's straight-edged map.",
   r"<strong>$T_\Omega\subseteq L_\Omega$ always,</strong> but a cusp makes the map over-approximate.",
   r"<strong>LICQ (independent wall normals) forces $T_\Omega=L_\Omega$,</strong> so the straight-line map is exact and KKT works."]),
1001: (r"What guarantees one flat wall can separate two airspaces?",
  [r"<strong>$A\cap B=\emptyset$, both convex:</strong> the airspaces do not overlap and have no dents.",
   r"<strong>$\sup_Aa^\top x\le\inf_Ba^\top y$:</strong> in the direction $a$, every point of $A$ is behind every point of $B$.",
   r"<strong>The wall is $a^\top x=\beta$ with $\beta$ between those two numbers.</strong>"]),
1002: (r"Why is there always a flat wall touching a convex airspace at its edge?",
  [r"<strong>$x_0\in\partial C$:</strong> you are standing on the boundary.",
   r"<strong>$a^\top x\le a^\top x_0$ for all $x\in C$:</strong> the whole airspace is on one side of the wall through $x_0$ with normal $a$.",
   r"<strong>It follows from separating $x_0$ from the interior of $C$.</strong>"]),
1003: (r"Either the walls can hold gravity or some heading proves they cannot. Which?",
  [r"<strong>$Ax=b,\ x\ge0$:</strong> gravity $b$ is a non-negative combination of the wall normals (columns of $A$), so the walls can hold it.",
   r"<strong>$A^\top y\ge0,\ b^\top y\lt0$:</strong> a heading $y$ no wall blocks along which gravity still drags you down.",
   r"<strong>Exactly one of the two holds:</strong> that heading is the certificate that the first story is impossible."]),
1007: (r"Which heading proves $b'=(0,1)$ cannot be held by the walls $a_1=(1,0),a_2=(1,1)$?",
  [r"<strong>$A^\top y=(1,0)\ge0$ for $y=(1,-1)$:</strong> neither wall blocks that heading ($a_2\cdot y=0$ is the edge of the cone).",
   r"<strong>$b'^\top y=-1\lt0$:</strong> gravity $b'$ still pulls along it.",
   r"<strong>So $b'$ is outside the cone,</strong> while $b=(2,1)=a_1+a_2$ sits inside it."]),
1004: (r"Either some heading frees the plane from every wall, or the pushes balance. Which?",
  [r"<strong>$Ax\lt0$:</strong> a heading that moves you strictly away from every wall at once: open sky.",
   r"<strong>$A^\top y=0,\ y\ge0,\ y\ne0$:</strong> the wall pushes cancel with non-negative weights: you are boxed in.",
   r"<strong>Exactly one of the two holds;</strong> this is how interior points and degenerate boxes are told apart."]),
1005: (r"Which pulls can never make a legal heading go downhill?",
  [r"<strong>$K^*=\{y:y^\top x\ge0\ \forall x\in K\}$:</strong> forces that never oppose any legal heading.",
   r"<strong>The polar cone $K^\circ=-K^*$</strong> is the set of pulls that make every legal heading go flat or uphill: at a minimum, $-\nabla f\in K^\circ$.",
   r"<strong>$(K^*)^*=K$ for closed convex $K$:</strong> taking the dual twice gets the original cone back."]),
1006: (r"Why must the wall pushes balance gravity at a constrained minimum?",
  [r"<strong>No legal heading is downhill:</strong> there is no $d$ with $\nabla f^\top d\lt0$ and $\nabla g_j^\top d\le0$.",
   r"<strong>Farkas turns that 'no heading' statement</strong> into $-\nabla f=\sum\mu_j\nabla g_j$ with $\mu_j\ge0$.",
   r"<strong>Reading it back:</strong> gravity is exactly cancelled by non-negative pushes from the walls you touch."]),
1101: (r"What four things must a parked plane satisfy?",
  [r"<strong>$\nabla f+\sum\lambda_i\nabla h_i+\sum\mu_j\nabla g_j=0$:</strong> gravity is cancelled by the wall pushes.",
   r"<strong>$g\le0$, $h=0$:</strong> you are inside the airspace.",
   r"<strong>$\mu_j\ge0$:</strong> walls only push, never pull. <strong>$\mu_jg_j=0$:</strong> a wall you are not touching pushes with zero."]),
1102: (r"Which two statements does $\mu_jg_j=0$ hide?",
  [r"<strong>$g_j\lt0\Rightarrow\mu_j=0$:</strong> a gap between wing and wall means no push (switch OFF).",
   r"<strong>$\mu_j\gt0\Rightarrow g_j=0$:</strong> a wall that is pushing must be touching (switch ON).",
   r"<strong>Multiplying them is a compact way to say 'never both'.</strong>"]),
1103: (r"When does a balanced parking spot have to be the global lowest one?",
  [r"<strong>$f,g_j$ convex and $h_i$ affine:</strong> a convex valley with convex airspace.",
   r"<strong>KKT then says a balanced point</strong> beats every other legal point: $f(x^*)\le f(x)$.",
   r"<strong>It works because the tangent plane at $x^*$ lies under the terrain,</strong> and the wall pushes cannot make it better."]),
1104: (r"What do the touching walls' normals have to look like for the pushes to be well defined?",
  [r"<strong>rank = $p+|\mathcal A|$:</strong> the normals of all active walls are linearly independent.",
   r"<strong>Independence means the push on each wall is uniquely determined</strong> by the force balance.",
   r"<strong>If two normals line up (a cusp),</strong> the pushes are not unique or may not exist and KKT can fail at a true minimum."]),
1105: (r"What kind of room does the airspace need for the dual floor to touch the lowest altitude?",
  [r"<strong>$g_j(\hat x)\lt0$ for all $j$:</strong> a point strictly inside every wall.",
   r"<strong>$A\hat x=b$:</strong> it also satisfies the equality walls.",
   r"<strong>With that room, $d^*=p^*$:</strong> zero duality gap for a convex program."]),
1106: (r"Which of the two guesses survives: floating free or pressed to the wall?",
  [r"<strong>$\nabla f=(2x_1,8x_2)$:</strong> the gravity pull; $\nabla g=(-1,-1)$ is the wall normal.",
   r"<strong>Case 1 ($\mu=0$):</strong> $\nabla f=0$ puts you at the origin, which breaks the wall constraint, so the guess fails.",
   r"<strong>Case 2 (on the wall):</strong> $x_1+x_2=1$ and $2x_1=8x_2$ give $x^*=(4/5,1/5)$ and $\mu^*=8/5\gt0$."]),
1201: (r"Along which directions must the terrain curve upward?",
  [r"<strong>$\nabla^2_{xx}\mathcal L$ = the Lagrangian's curvature:</strong> the terrain's curvature plus the walls' curvature weighted by their pushes.",
   r"<strong>$d\in M(x^*)$ = the runway:</strong> directions that keep all touching walls fixed.",
   r"<strong>$d^\top\nabla^2\mathcal Ld\ge0$ on the runway only:</strong> off the runway the ground may drop and it does not matter."]),
1202: (r"What turns the necessary curvature test into a guarantee?",
  [r"<strong>Strict $\gt0$ for every non-zero critical direction:</strong> the runway curves strictly upward under the wheels.",
   r"<strong>Critical cone = directions that neither gain nor lose altitude to first order.</strong>",
   r"<strong>Together with the KKT conditions this proves a strict local minimum.</strong>"]),
1203: (r"How can the pilot test the runway's curvature without parametrising the runway?",
  [r"<strong>The bordered matrix puts the wall normals around $\nabla^2\mathcal L$:</strong> $B=\begin{bmatrix}0&J\\J^\top&\nabla^2\mathcal L\end{bmatrix}$.",
   r"<strong>Its trailing principal minors carry the sign of the curvature restricted to the runway.</strong>",
   r"<strong>The rule $\operatorname{sign}\det=(-1)^p$</strong> (for $p$ walls) says the runway dips: a minimum."]),
1204: (r"When is the critical cone a subspace and when is it a wedge?",
  [r"<strong>Every touching wall pushing ($\mu_j\gt0$):</strong> each wall's inequality becomes an equality, so the critical cone is a subspace (all directions that slide along all walls).",
   r"<strong>A touching wall pushing zero ($\mu_j=0$):</strong> you may peel away from it in one direction, which adds a half-space and gives a proper cone.",
   r"<strong>In 2D with two strictly pushing walls the only legal direction is zero.</strong>"]),
1205: (r"What curvature actually matters on a banked runway: the terrain's or the Lagrangian's?",
  [r"<strong>$\dot x^\top\nabla^2f\,\dot x$:</strong> the terrain's own curvature along your velocity.",
   r"<strong>$\nabla f^\top\ddot x$:</strong> the extra term because your path is curved (a bank); at a KKT point it equals $\sum\mu_j\nabla g_j^\top\ddot x$.",
   r"<strong>Adding them gives $d^\top[\nabla^2f+\sum\mu_j\nabla^2g_j]d$:</strong> the Lagrangian's curvature on the runway."]),
1206: (r"Does the sign of the bordered determinant confirm a minimum at the south-west point?",
  [r"<strong>$B$'s border is the ring's normal;</strong> the inner block is the Lagrangian's Hessian at $x^*$.",
   r"<strong>$\det B=-4\sqrt2\lt0=(-1)^1$</strong> with one constraint ($p=1$) is exactly the sign pattern for a strict local minimum.",
   r"<strong>If it were positive,</strong> the south-west point would be a maximum."]),
1301: (r"Where does the nearest legal point sit relative to the plane and the rest of the airspace?",
  [r"<strong>$x-p$ = the vector from the nearest legal spot to the plane.</strong>",
   r"<strong>$\langle x-p,y-p\rangle\le0$ for every legal $y$:</strong> the angle at $p$ between that vector and any other legal point is obtuse or right.",
   r"<strong>That is the entire characterisation of the projection:</strong> $p\in C$ and the inequality hold iff $p=\Pi_C(x)$."]),
1302: (r"Why can a projection never pull two planes farther apart?",
  [r"<strong>$\|\Pi x-\Pi y\|^2+\|(I-\Pi)x-(I-\Pi)y\|^2\le\|x-y\|^2$:</strong> the along-the-wall and across-the-wall parts together are at most the original distance squared.",
   r"<strong>Dropping the second (non-negative) term</strong> gives $\|\Pi x-\Pi y\|\le\|x-y\|$.",
   r"<strong>That is what makes projected methods stable:</strong> the correction never amplifies errors."]),
1303: (r"How do you drop a plane onto a wall along its normal?",
  [r"<strong>$a^\top x-b$ = how far past the wall the plane is,</strong> measured along the normal direction (in units of $\|a\|$).",
   r"<strong>Dividing by $\|a\|^2$ and multiplying by $a$</strong> turns that signed excess into the displacement back along the normal.",
   r"<strong>The half-space version has $\max(0,\cdot)$:</strong> if you are already on the legal side, the excess is 0 and you stay put."]),
1307: (r"What do the box and the ball do to a plane that is outside?",
  [r"<strong>Box:</strong> $\min(u_i,\max(l_i,x_i))$ clamps each coordinate to its own limits independently.",
   r"<strong>Ball:</strong> $x/\max(1,\|x\|/R)$ leaves you alone inside the ball and scales you back along the ray to the centre once outside.",
   r"<strong>Both are one-line projections:</strong> cheap to compute, which is why projected gradient uses them."]),
1304: (r"How does Moreau split any wind vector into 'with the airspace' and 'against the wall'?",
  [r"<strong>$x_1=\Pi_K(x)\in K$:</strong> the part that points into the legal cone.",
   r"<strong>$x_2=\Pi_{K^\circ}(x)\in K^\circ$:</strong> the part that points out of it.",
   r"<strong>$\langle x_1,x_2\rangle=0$:</strong> the two parts are perpendicular; in 1D this is $x=x_++x_-$."]),
1305: (r"What does Dykstra's memory do that plain alternating projection does not?",
  [r"<strong>$y_k=\Pi_{C_1}(x_k+p_{k-1})$:</strong> project onto the first airspace, but first add back the correction remembered from last time.",
   r"<strong>$p_k=x_k+p_{k-1}-y_k$:</strong> remember what this projection moved you sideways by.",
   r"<strong>Without these memory vectors you end at some point of the overlap;</strong> with them you end at the nearest point of the overlap."]),
1306: (r"How do you find the water level $\tau$ that makes the leftover fuel sum to one?",
  [r"<strong>$x-\tau\mathbf1$ shaves the same level off every tank;</strong> $\max(0,\cdot)$ lets empty tanks stay empty.",
   r"<strong>$\tau=\frac{\sum_{i\le k}x_{(i)}-1}{k}$:</strong> the extra over 1 in the $k$ largest tanks, shared equally ($\frac{2.5-1}{2}=0.75$).",
   r"<strong>Result $(0.75,0.25,0,0)$ sums to 1 and is the nearest point of the simplex.</strong>"]),
}
FIX = {cid: {"hint": h, "breakdown": b} for cid, (h, b) in B.items()}
